 "drivingQuestion": "Is food preparation the only difference between Yom Tov and Shabbat?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "resolved in beitzah.sugya209",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "beitzah.37a.9",
   "daf": "37a",
   "sentenceId": 8,
   "he": "כׇּל אֵלּוּ בְּיוֹם טוֹב אָמְרוּ, וּרְמִינְהוּ: מַשִּׁילִין דֶּרֶךְ אֲרוּבָּה בְּיוֹם טוֹב, אֲבָל לֹא בְּשַׁבָּת!",
   "en": "§ It was taught in the mishna: The Sages «spoke» of «all these» acts being prohibited even «with regard to a Festival;» all the more so are they prohibited on Shabbat. There is no difference between a Festival and Shabbat except for work involving food. The Gemara «raises a contradiction» against this from an earlier mishna: «One may lower» produce from the roof into the house «through a skylight» to prevent it from being spoiled by the rain «on a Festival, but not on Shabbat.» This shows that there is another difference between a Festival and Shabbat besides food preparation: Doing a strenuous activity to prevent a loss is permitted on a Festival but prohibited on Shabbat."
  }
 ],
